       Mouse events from _x_t_e_r_m are _n_o_t ignored in cooked  mode  if  they  have
       been  enabled  by  mmoouusseemmaasskk.  Instead, the _x_t_e_r_m mouse report sequence
       appears in the string read.

       Mouse event reports from _x_t_e_r_m are not detected correctly in  a  window
       with  keypad application mode disabled, since they are interpreted as a
       variety of function key.  Set the terminal's _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o capability  kkmmoouuss
       to  “\E[M” (the beginning of the response from _x_t_e_r_m for mouse clicks).
       Other values of kkmmoouuss are permitted under the same assumption, that is,
       the report begins with that sequence.

       Because there are no standard response sequences that serve to identify
       terminals supporting the _x_t_e_r_m mouse protocol, _n_c_u_r_s_e_s assumes that  if
       kkmmoouuss is defined in the terminal description, or if the terminal type's
       primary name or aliases contain the string “xterm”, then  the  terminal
       may send mouse events.  The kkmmoouuss capability is checked first, allowing
       use of newer _x_t_e_r_m mouse protocols, such as its private mode 1006.

